+            # tensor_model_parallel_size == 1 with rosenthal) are RETIRED: 0017 replaced the
+            # potential-form soft loss, which needed a size-1 reduce group, with a synced-
+            # coefficient construction (a detached, globally-reduced marginal cost multiplying a
+            # still-local, still-differentiable carrier -- see _apply_rosenthal_loss) that is
+            # correct at any reduce-group size. Numbers 5 and 6 are retired, not reused: they are
+            # pinned into test names elsewhere in this codebase
+            # (pretrain_config_test.py, this file's own test module), and renaming eight tests to
+            # renumber the two survivors below would buy nothing. The survivors keep rules 1-4,
+            # 7, 8.
+            #
+            # Sanity bound on the congestion pressure at full imbalance -- not a correctness
+            # condition, so this warns rather than blocking what may be a deliberate high-lambda
+            # experiment. Only evaluated when moe_aux_loss_coeff is a plain scalar: the list form
+            # (one coefficient per entry of a list-valued moe_router_load_balancing_type) is out
+            # of scope for the congestion loss, which only ever selects one balancing type. The
+            # bound expression itself lives in losses/cost_families.py, imported here and by our
+            # own launcher's copy of this check, so it is written out in exactly one place --
+            # pressure_bound returns the expression string alongside the value (not just the
+            # value) so neither caller re-derives which branch (hard's capped E/K vs soft's
+            # uncapped E) produced it by testing the variant a second time; a caller that got that
+            # second branch wrong would print a plausible but wrong expression next to a correct
+            # number, in a warning string no test asserts on.
+            if isinstance(self.moe_aux_loss_coeff, (int, float)):
+                _p = cost_exponent(self.moe_rosenthal_cost)
+                _bound = pressure_bound(
+                    self.moe_aux_loss_coeff,
+                    self.moe_rosenthal_lambda,
+                    self.num_moe_experts,
+                    self.moe_router_topk,
+                    self.moe_rosenthal_cost,
+                    self.moe_rosenthal_variant,
+                    num_experts_name="num_moe_experts",
+                )
+                if _bound.value > 1:
+                    warnings.warn(
+                        "moe_rosenthal_lambda * moe_aux_loss_coeff * "
+                        f"{_bound.expr}**{_p} = {_bound.value:.4g} > 1 -- the "
+                        "congestion pressure at full imbalance exceeds the sanity bound; not an "
+                        "error, but check moe_rosenthal_lambda and moe_aux_loss_coeff are what "
+                        "you intend."
+                    )
+
+        # Rule 7 is about the NON-rosenthal case (a stray --moe-rosenthal-log-grad-ratio on an
+        # aux_loss/sinkhorn/none run), so it must sit outside the "is it rosenthal?" branch above --
+        # nested inside it, this check would never fire on exactly the run it exists to catch. Same
+        # list-vs-scalar fix as the guard above: a list selecting a rosenthal type alongside another
+        # type must not be rejected here either.
+        #
+        # Requires the selection to be EXACTLY one rosenthal type, not merely to include one:
+        # accepting ["seq_aux_loss", "rosenthal"] would be wrong because the "cg" gradient probe
+        # (the router-grad-probe patch) wraps the ONE `logits` tensor that feeds
+        # compute_routing_scores_for_aux_loss, and every active aux loss type reads that same
+        # tensor -- so with a mixed list, rosenthal_grad_norm_cg would silently record the
+        # COMBINED gradient of every active aux loss, not the congestion loss's own contribution,
+        # with no error (that metric is the numerator of the Exp 3 gradient-share ratio).
+        if self.moe_rosenthal_log_grad_ratio:
+            # selected_rosenthal_types owns the scalar/list normalization and the ROSENTHAL_TYPES
+            # membership test (see its docstring and the guard above, which shares it); the only
+            # thing this rule needs beyond that is whether the field selects anything ELSE besides
